Exercise: Running total number of employees hired by department name and by year, ordered by year.

Solution: We used an INNER JOIN to connect the Department and EmployeeDepartmentHistory tables, and an INNER JOIN to connect those to the Employee table.

To calculate the running total we used the SUM Window Function. We calculate a cumulative running total, by adding 1 for each row processed for each department and for each year separately, starting from the very first row of your dataset (or partition) up to the current row.

The SUM(1) OVER (PARTITION BY Departments.[Name], YEAR(Employees.[HireDate]) ORDER BY YEAR(Employees.[HireDate]) ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING) is explained here.
a) SUM(1): Acts as a counter for each row processed.

b) The OVER clause has the following arguments:
b.1) PARTITION BY: divides the query result set into partitions.
The window function is applied to each partition separately and computation restarts for each partition.
If you don't specify PARTITION BY, the function treats all rows of the query result set as a single partition.

b.2) ORDER BY: defines the logical order of the rows within each partition of the result set.
Meaning, it specifies the logical order in which the window function calculation is performed.

b.3) ROWS or RANGE: limits the rows within the partition by specifying start and end points within the partition. 
It requires ORDER BY argument and the default value is from the start of partition to the current element if the ORDER BY argument is specified.

For this exercise, the arguments for the OVER clause are as follows:
b.1) PARTITION BY: department Name and YEAR of the HireDate
b.2) ORDER BY: YEAR of the HireDate
b.3) ROWS or RANGE: ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING. Meaning, we tell SQL Server that the window starts at the first row of the partition.


Tables used
[AdventureWorks2022].[HumanResources].[Department]
[AdventureWorks2022].[HumanResources].[EmployeeDepartmentHistory]
[AdventureWorks2022].[HumanResources].[Employee]

Functions used
SUM(1) OVER a window function
YEAR function


USE AdventureWorks2022;
GO

SELECT Departments.[Name]
      ,YEAR(Employees.[HireDate]) AS YearHired
      ,SUM(1) OVER (PARTITION BY Departments.[Name], YEAR(Employees.[HireDate]) ORDER BY YEAR(Employees.[HireDate]) ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING) AS RunningTotalHired
FROM [AdventureWorks2022].[HumanResources].[Department] AS Departments
INNER JOIN [AdventureWorks2022].[HumanResources].[EmployeeDepartmentHistory] AS EmployeesHistorical
ON Departments.[DepartmentID] = EmployeesHistorical.[DepartmentID]
INNER JOIN [AdventureWorks2022].[HumanResources].[Employee] AS Employees
ON EmployeesHistorical.[BusinessEntityID] = Employees.[BusinessEntityID]


